1. Positioning vs Competitors

Positioning = Where do you want your product to stand in the customer's mind compared with competitors?

Imagine there are 3 food-delivery apps:

App A: Cheapest delivery
App B: Fastest delivery
App C: Best restaurant selection

They are all solving the same basic problem, but each has a different position.

Simple formula

For [target customer], our product is [category] that provides [main benefit], unlike [competitor], because [reason].

Example:

For students, our app is a free study assistant that gives simple explanations, unlike general AI tools, because it is specifically designed around their syllabus.

That's positioning.

2. Differentiation

Differentiation = What makes your product meaningfully different or better?

Suppose you build a text-to-audio product.

There are already competitors.

Simply saying:

"We convert text to audio."

isn't differentiation because competitors can do that too.

Instead:

"Free text-to-audio with no subscription, simple controls, and easy downloads."

Now you're creating differences.

Types of differentiation

Product

Better features
Better quality
Easier interface

Price

Cheaper
Free
Better pricing model

Experience

Easier to use
Faster
Better customer support

Niche

Designed specifically for a particular group

For example:

"A project-management tool specifically for college student teams."

That's differentiation through focus.

3. Competitive Moat / Defensive Strategy

This is the part people often find confusing.

A moat is something that makes your competitive advantage difficult for competitors to copy.

Think about a castle 🏰.

The castle has a moat around it.

Competitors can see the castle, but the moat makes attacking it harder.

Same idea in business.

Example

You create an app with a beautiful UI.

Competitor sees it and copies the UI.

No moat.

You create an app with:

10 million users
years of user data
strong network effects
a trusted brand
proprietary technology
huge distribution advantage

Now copying the UI isn't enough.

That's a defensive advantage.

Common types of moats

1. Network effects

More users → more valuable product.

Example:

A social network.

If only 10 people use it, it's not very useful.

If 100 million people use it, leaving becomes difficult.

2. Brand

People trust your brand.

A competitor can copy your product, but not instantly copy years of brand reputation.

3. Switching costs

It becomes inconvenient or expensive for customers to leave.

Example:

A company has all its data, workflows and employees set up inside a software platform.

Moving to another platform takes significant effort.

4. Data

Your product improves because you've accumulated valuable data.

A new competitor doesn't have that data immediately.

5. Technology

You have technology, patents, algorithms, infrastructure, or expertise that is difficult to reproduce.

6. Economies of scale

You become cheaper as you grow.

A huge company might produce something for ₹50 while a small competitor needs ₹80.

4. Price vs Value

This is one of the most important concepts.

There are basically two major ways to compete:

Strategy A — Compete on PRICE

You say:

"We'll give you a similar solution for less money."

Example:

Competitor → ₹999/month
Your product → ₹299/month

Your advantage is cost.

But there's a problem:

Your competitor can reduce their price too.

Then you reduce yours again.

Eventually:

₹999 → ₹299 → ₹199 → ₹99

This can become a price war.

Strategy B — Compete on VALUE

Instead of being cheaper, you provide something customers value more.

Example:

Competitor:

₹500/month — basic analytics

Your product:

₹800/month — advanced analytics + AI insights + automation + better support

You're more expensive.

But customers think:

"I'm getting much more value."

That's value-based competition.

5. Price vs Value — Simple Example

Imagine two cafés.

Café A

Coffee = ₹50

Basic coffee.

Café B

Coffee = ₹180

But:

Better coffee
Better environment
Faster service
Better experience
Strong brand

People still buy Café B.

Why?

Because they're not only buying coffee.

They're buying value.

6. The Important Connection

These four concepts actually connect together:

Positioning
↓
Where are we in the market?

Differentiation
↓
Why are we different?

Value
↓
Why should customers choose us?

Moat
↓
Why can't competitors easily copy us?

Think of it like this:

Positioning → Differentiation → Value → Moat

Example:

Product: Student AI Assistant

Positioning

"AI assistant specifically for college students."

Differentiation

Syllabus-based explanations + notes + quizzes.

Value

Students save time studying.

Moat

Over time, you build a large educational dataset, integrations with universities, strong brand, and user network.

Now you have an actual competitive strategy.

7. When should you compete on Price?

Choose price when:

Customers are highly price-sensitive
Products are relatively similar
You can operate at lower cost
You have economies of scale
Customers don't care much about additional features

Example:

Generic commodity products.

8. When should you compete on Value?

Choose value when:

Customers care about quality
You solve an important problem
Your product has unique features
You can create a better experience
Customers are willing to pay for the improvement

For startups, differentiating on value is often more attractive than simply trying to be the cheapest, because constantly undercutting established competitors can be difficult.

The whole topic in one example

Imagine you're launching a college productivity app.

Competitors

Notion, Google Docs, generic AI tools.

Positioning

"A productivity platform built specifically for college students."

Differentiation
College timetable
Assignment tracker
AI study assistant
Exam planner
Value

Students manage their entire academic life in one place.

Price

Free basic version + ₹99/month premium.

Moat

Over time:

University integrations
Student network
Educational data
Strong student community
Brand

Now competitors can't simply copy your homepage and destroy your advantage.

Remember these 4 questions

Whenever you're analyzing a competitor, ask:

1. Positioning:

Where are we compared with competitors?

2. Differentiation:

Why are we different?

3. Value:

Why should customers care/pay?

4. Moat:

Why can't competitors easily copy us?

That's the core of this entire topic.
